insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 1, 'Every function in the QRA engine answers a call in one of two shapes. Which two?', '["A result object carrying a `basis` block, or the same result with the offending input quietly replaced by a default the engine holds for it.", "A result object with a list of warnings attached whenever an input looks doubtful, so that a figure always comes back to the analyst.", "A result object carrying a `basis` block, or an object with `error` and `field`, where `field` names the offending input.", "A bare number per year for the quantity asked, or zero whenever an input cannot be used at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says every function returns either a result object carrying a `basis` block or an object with `error` and `field`, and `field` names the offending input. The engine invents no number, so it has no default to slip in. It never hands back a figure with a warning beside it, and a refused call never comes back as zero, which a reader would take for a real frequency.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 2, 'Which inputs does the engine fill in by itself when the analyst leaves them out?', '["None: every scenario frequency, branch probability, probability of death and occupancy is an input the analyst supplies.", "The probability of death at each place, which it works out from the scenario frequency and the distance to the release point.", "A generic initiating frequency for each kind of release, from failure data it carries.", "The occupancy of each place, which it takes as the whole year of 8760 hours whenever the roster is left blank."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine invents no number: every scenario frequency, branch probability, probability of death, occupancy, VPF, DF and rate is an input. A probability of death is a stated input that belongs to the consequence course, so the engine never derives one from a distance. It carries no failure data, and 8760 only converts stated hours into a fraction of the year; it is never an occupancy the engine assumes.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 3, 'The engine exports three functions, `toxicPlumeGridPointRisk`, `thermalFatalityTransect` and `poolFireFatalityTransect`, that run the consequence engine. What does this course do with them?', '["It teaches their inputs at Associate level, so that each learner can produce every probability of death that an LSIR sum needs.", "It runs them behind every capstone, so that no probability of death in any capstone is ever typed in by the person working it.", "It uses the toxic one alone, since a toxic cloud is the only effect that a quantitative risk assessment has to model for itself at every place it studies.", "It never asks for their inputs, because making a probability of death is consequence modelling and belongs to the consequence course."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest says the three functions MAKE a probability of death and that this course does not teach their inputs; that work is consequence modelling and belongs to the consequence course. So no Associate lesson teaches them, and a capstone states each probability of death it uses. A fire or an explosion is modelled in the consequence course just as a toxic cloud is, so none of the three effects is special here.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 4, 'An analyst types 2 as a scenario frequency per year and 2 as a probability of death for the same scenario, named ''fire'', at one place. What does the engine do with each?', '["It accepts both, since it checks only that each number is 0 or more before it forms the product for the place.", "It accepts the frequency, which counts events per year and may exceed one, and refuses the probability of death, which must lie from 0 to 1.", "It refuses both, since no input anywhere in the engine may take a value above one, frequencies included.", "It caps the probability of death at one and refuses the frequency as more than one event a year at a single place."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The units table says a frequency counts events per year and may exceed one, while a probability lies from zero to one. So the frequency is accepted and the probability of death is refused in the engine''s own words: ''fire'' must be a probability of death in [0, 1]. The engine never checks only for a sign, never caps a value it cannot use, and never limits a frequency to one.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 5, 'How does the digest read a frequency of 1e-4 per year?', '["The probability that the event happens in any single year, which is the same number written in a different unit.", "One event in ten thousand years on average: a rate, kept in a different input from any probability.", "The probability of death of a person standing at the release point for one year.", "One event in a thousand years on average, the exponent counting the zeros in the number of years between events."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest says a frequency of 1e-4 per year is one event in ten thousand years on average, and that it is a rate and never a probability, which the engine keeps in different inputs. A probability of death is a separate stated input that belongs to the consequence course. Reading the exponent as a thousand years miscounts it by a factor of ten.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 6, 'The engine turns hours spent at a place into a fraction of the year. What does it divide by?', '["8766, the hours of an average calendar year, so that the leap years in a working life are counted in every person''s occupancy.", "Nothing, since the hours are carried straight into the individual risk per annum as hours with no conversion made.", "The hours on that person''s own roster, whatever the calendar says.", "8760, the H3 engine''s HOURS_PER_YEAR, so 8760 hours at one place is an occupancy of 1.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hours convert at 8760 hours a year, the H3 engine''s HOURS_PER_YEAR, and 8760 hours at one place is an occupancy of 1.000000. Dividing by 8766 is a wrong method the digest measures: it moves the EREMOR operator''s figure away from the engine''s. Occupancy is always a fraction of the calendar year, so hours are never carried unconverted, and a roster is never the denominator.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 7, 'An event tree is passed with an empty branch set. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["tree.branches: must be a non-empty list of { name, probability, next? }", "tree: an event tree needs at least one outcome before a leaf frequency can be formed from the initiating frequency", "initiatingFrequencyPerYr: must be a frequency above 0 per year", "tree.branches[0].name: every branch needs a name"]'::jsonb, 0, 'That is the engine''s own message for an empty branch set, from the refusal table, and it names the field `tree.branches`. The initiating frequency message and the missing name message are real engine words for two other calls in that table. No refusal in the table speaks of an outcome needed before a leaf frequency.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 8, 'A scenario named ''fire'' reaches `locationIndividualRisk` with a probability of death above one. Which field does the engine name?', '["`scenarios[0].frequencyPerYr`, saying ''fire'' must have a frequency of 0 or more per year before any product is formed.", "`scenarios`, saying the list must be a non-empty list of scenarios once the bad scenario is taken out of it.", "`scenarios[0].fatalityProbability`, the position of the offending scenario and the input within it.", "`fatalityProbability`, after capping it at one and adding a note to the basis."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The refusal table names `scenarios[0].fatalityProbability` and quotes the engine: ''fire'' must be a probability of death in [0, 1]. The frequency message belongs to a negative frequency and the non-empty list message to a call with no scenarios. A refusal names the offending input down to its position, and the engine never caps an input and carries on.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 9, 'A roster row for ''deck'' gives both an occupancy fraction and hours per year. What comes back?', '["An individual risk per annum built from the hours alone, since hours are the more exact of the two ways of stating a roster.", "An individual risk per annum built from the fraction alone, with the hours kept in the result for the reviewer to compare.", "locations[0].hoursPerYr: ''deck'' must lie in [0, 8760] hours", "locations[0].occupancyFraction: ''deck'': give exactly one of occupancyFraction and hoursPerYr"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the engine''s own words for both a fraction and hours on one row, from the refusal table. The engine does not choose between the two, so no figure is built from either. The message about [0, 8760] hours is real engine text for a different call, where more hours are given than a year holds.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 10, 'A roster puts one person at 0.6 of the year on the process deck and 0.5 of it in the control room. What does the engine do?', '["It refuses on `locations`: the occupancy fractions sum to 1.1, and one person cannot spend more than the whole year across locations.", "It scales both fractions down in proportion until they sum to one, and reports in the basis that it has done so for the reviewer.", "It accepts the roster, since each fraction on its own lies inside 0 to 1 and the engine checks each place separately.", "It keeps 0.6 for the process deck and trims the control room to 0.4, the part of the year left over."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The refusal table quotes the engine: the occupancy fractions sum to 1.1, and one person cannot spend more than the whole year across locations. A person is in one place at a time, so the total is checked as well as each place. The engine alters no input, so it neither rescales the fractions nor trims one of them.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 11, 'How many refusals does the digest table, across how many functions, and what number does a refusal carry?', '["138 refusals across 13 functions, one for each golden record the independent oracle wrote.", "39 refusals across 12 functions, and each carries the partial result formed before the bad input was read.", "39 refusals across 13 functions, and a refusal carries no number of its own.", "39 refusals across 13 functions, each carrying the offending value rounded to twelve decimals."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says 39 refusals are tabled across 13 functions, and that a refusal carries no number of its own. 138 is the count of golden records, which are cases the oracle wrote and are no refusals. A refusal returns only `error` and `field`, so it carries neither a partial result nor a rounded copy of the input.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 12, 'An analyst passes `constructor` as the substance class to `pbDirectIgnitionProbability`. What does the engine do?', '["It returns the lookup of the first class in its table, since a name that every JavaScript object carries is found on the lookup.", "It refuses on `substance` with the message any unknown class gets, listing k1-liquid, gas-low-reactivity and gas-average-high-reactivity.", "It returns a probability of 0 and a band of null, since the name it was handed has no row of its own in Table 4.5.", "It refuses on `releaseType`, reading a name that every object carries as a release type."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A preset or class name is looked up only among the names the engine itself defines, so `constructor` is refused like any other unknown name, and the refusal table shows the same `substance` message for it. The engine returns no lookup and no zero for a name it does not define, and the field it names is the substance, where the bad name was typed.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 13, 'Which of these does this course COMPUTE, as the seam table puts it?', '["A scenario frequency, from a release frequency and an event tree.", "A probability of death at a place, from a consequence model that this course builds for each fire and explosion.", "Who is where and for how long, read by the engine from the plot plan and the roster.", "A value of preventing a fatality, taken from published guidance that the engine holds as its own default figure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The seam table says this course computes a scenario frequency from a release frequency and an event tree. A probability of death at a place comes from a consequence model and belongs to the consequence course, so it is a stated input here. Occupancy is a stated input too, and VPF has no default at all.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 14, 'The vocabulary section legislates how the word "risk" is written in this course. What is the rule?', '["Written bare for the product of a frequency and a probability of death, since that is what the word means in this course.", "Written as it is in the risk and change course, where the word names a cell score on a matrix that this course takes over.", "Left bare whenever the same sentence already gives a unit per year, since the unit qualifies the word by itself.", "Always qualified: individual risk, LSIR, IRPA or societal risk."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The vocabulary section says the word is always qualified: individual risk, LSIR, IRPA or societal risk. It already means a matrix score in the risk and change course and a cost in the economics courses, and this course never scores a matrix cell, so a bare word would carry the wrong meaning. A unit per year does not name which quantity is meant.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-qra-answers', 15, 'Which job does the digest say the engine does NOT do?', '["Summing each scenario frequency times its probability of death at one place to give the LSIR there.", "Converting the hours a person spends at a place into a fraction of the year, at 8760 hours a year.", "Grid or wind rose bookkeeping of a full assessment; the caller supplies each scenario frequency and probability of death.", "Refusing a branch set whose probabilities fail to sum to one within its tolerance of 1e-9, and naming the failing set by its path."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest lists what the engine does not do, and grid or wind rose bookkeeping is on that list: the caller supplies each scenario frequency and probability of death. The LSIR sum, the conversion of hours at 8760 hours a year and the branch sum check within BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE of 1e-9 are all things the engine does.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 1, 'The engine''s model string for an event tree gives one rule for a leaf. What is it?', '["leaf frequency = f0 x sum of the branch probabilities on its path", "leaf frequency = f0 x product of the branch probabilities on its path; each branch set sums to 1", "leaf frequency = f0 x the probability of the last branch on its path", "leaf frequency = product of the branch probabilities on its path, f0 left out"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s model string, verbatim, is "event tree: leaf frequency = f0 x product of the branch probabilities on its path; each branch set sums to 1". Summing the probabilities on a path has no meaning, and the engine scales nothing. Every branch on the path multiplies in, the last one included, and the initiating frequency multiplies every leaf.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 2, 'The EREMOR tank overfill starts at 2e-3 per year (stated). On the path high level alarm missed then bund contains the spill then ignited in the bund, what leaf frequency does the engine return?', '["0.000014500000 per year, the pool fire total, since this leaf already carries every pool fire in the tree with it.", "0.000007500000 per year, which is the leaf through the overtopped bund, ignited outside the bund.", "0.000063000000 per year, the leaf not ignited in the bund.", "0.000007000000 per year, from a path probability of 0.003500000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine gives this leaf a path probability of 0.003500000000 and a frequency of 0.000007000000 per year, the initiating frequency times the product of the three branch probabilities on its path. 0.000007500000 is the other pool fire leaf, through the overtopped bund, and 0.000014500000 is the two pooled together. 0.000063000000 is the neighbouring leaf where the spill in the bund does not ignite.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 3, 'Two leaves of the overfill tree carry the outcome ''pool fire''. What total does the engine report for that outcome?', '["0.000014500000 per year, the two leaves of 0.000007000000 and 0.000007500000 pooled into one outcome.", "0.000007000000 per year, the first pool fire leaf, the second dropped as a duplicate.", "0.000007500000 per year, the larger of the two pool fire leaves, since one outcome keeps only its worst leaf in the total.", "0.000085500000 per year, the fire leaves added to the leaves never ignited."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine totals every leaf that shares an outcome, so the two pool fire leaves, 0.000007000000 and 0.000007500000, pool into 0.000014500000 per year. Reading only the first leaf gives 0.000007000000 and misses the leaf through the overtopped bund, and keeping the larger alone drops the other. 0.000085500000 is the separate ''no fire'' total.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 4, 'What do all the leaves of the EREMOR overfill tree sum back to?', '["0.001900000000 per year, since the leaf where the high level alarm is answered releases nothing and falls out of the tree.", "0.000085500000 per year, since only the leaves with no fire count.", "0.002000000000 per year, the initiating frequency, because each branch set sums to one.", "0.000014500000 per year, since only the leaves that end in a fire are counted as outcomes of the tree."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every branch set is exhaustive and exclusive, so the leaves sum back to the initiating frequency, 0.002000000000 per year. The ''no release'' leaf is a leaf like any other, 0.001900000000 per year, and it is kept. The pool fire total of 0.000014500000 and the ''no fire'' total of 0.000085500000 are each one outcome among three.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 5, 'On the overfill path high level alarm missed then bund overtopped then ignited outside the bund, what path probability does the engine print?', '["0.003500000000, which the engine prints for the path through the bund that contains the spill and then ignites inside it", "0.011250000000, which the engine prints for the path on which the spill overtops the bund and is never ignited", "0.003750000000", "0.031500000000, the path contained in the bund and never ignited"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine prints 0.003750000000 for this path, the product of the branch probabilities on it, and its leaf frequency is 0.000007500000 per year. 0.003500000000 belongs to the contained path that ignites in the bund, 0.011250000000 to the overtopped path that never ignites, and 0.031500000000 to the contained path that never ignites.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 6, 'A leaf in a tree is given no `outcome` of its own. What does the engine call it in the totals?', '["Its branch name.", "The outcome of the leaf just above it on the same branch set, carried down so that every leaf in the tree has a named outcome.", "Nothing: a leaf with no outcome is left out of the totals and its frequency is dropped from the tree without a message.", "The word ''unnamed'', with a note in the basis that a name was missing when the tree was read."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest says a leaf with no `outcome` of its own takes its branch name. Nothing is borrowed from a neighbouring leaf, no frequency is dropped, and the engine writes no placeholder name. A branch with no name at all is a different matter: the engine refuses it.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 7, 'A branch set is typed as 0.6 and 0.3. What does the engine say, in its own words?', '["tree.branches: the missing 0.1 has been added to the last branch in the set so that the branch probabilities sum to 1 as every branch set must", "tree.branches[1].probability: the second branch must be a probability in [0, 1], and the branch set has been scaled to close", "tree.branches: a branch set of two must be exhaustive, so the second branch probability is read as one minus the first", "tree.branches: the branch probabilities sum to 0.8999999999999999, not 1 (tolerance 1e-9): every branch set must be exhaustive and exclusive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'These are the engine''s own words, from the refusal table, and the branch set is refused. The engine alters no input, so it neither adds the gap to a branch, scales the set nor reads a second branch as one minus the first. Each probability here lies inside [0, 1]; the fault is the sum.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 8, '0.7, 0.2 and 0.1 sum to one on paper and to 0.9999999999999999 in IEEE double. What does the engine do with that branch set?', '["It refuses it, since 0.9999999999999999 falls short of exactly 1.", "It accepts it, because the absolute tolerance of 1e-9 covers the gap left by double arithmetic.", "It accepts it after rounding each probability to twelve decimals.", "It refuses it and names the third branch as the one that falls short."]'::jsonb, 1, 'BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE is 1e-9, absolute, and the digest''s table shows 0.7, 0.2 and 0.1 accepted although they sum to 0.9999999999999999 in double. Demanding an exact 1 in double would refuse a set that is exact on paper. The engine rounds no input, and it names no single branch for a sum.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 9, 'Someone enters 0.4000001 and 0.6 as the two probabilities under one node. How is that handled, and why?', '["Refuses it: the sum is 1.0000001, a tenth of a millionth over one, which is a typing error far outside the tolerance of 1e-9.", "Accepts it, since a sum a tenth of a millionth over one is the same kind of gap as the double rounding in 0.7, 0.2 and 0.1.", "Accepts it after trimming the first branch back to 0.4, so that the branch set closes and the tree can be run.", "Refuses it for the first branch alone, since 0.4000001 carries more decimals than a branch probability may be typed with."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sum is 1.0000001, and the engine refuses the set in its own words: the branch probabilities sum to 1.0000001, not 1 (tolerance 1e-9). A gap of a tenth of a millionth is far wider than the 1e-9 that covers double arithmetic, so it is a typing error. The engine trims no branch, and it sets no limit on the decimals a probability may carry.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 10, 'A lower branch set, two levels down a tree, sums to 0.5. How does the refusal say which node failed?', '["It names only `tree.branches`, the top of the tree, and leaves the analyst to search every lower set for the one that failed.", "It names the initiating frequency, since a tree that does not close cannot give leaves that sum back to it.", "It names none: a lower set is closed for the analyst by the engine.", "It names the branch set by its path, `tree.branches[0].next.branches`, and says the probabilities sum to 0.5."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest says a refusal names the branch set by its path, so a deep tree says which node failed; the refusal table shows `tree.branches[0].next.branches` with the sum of 0.5. The engine does not stop at the top of the tree, it does not blame the initiating frequency, and it closes no set on the analyst''s behalf.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 11, 'One branch set holds two branches both named ''fire''. What does the engine return?', '["A tree in which the two ''fire'' branches are merged into one, their probabilities added before any leaf is formed", "tree.branches[1].name: ''fire'' appears twice in one branch set", "A tree in which the second ''fire'' leaf is renamed with a number so that both leaves can be carried into the totals", "tree.branches[0].name: every branch needs a name"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table, naming the second branch, `tree.branches[1].name`. The engine merges nothing and renames nothing: a repeated name within one branch set is refused. The missing name message is real engine text for a branch that has no name at all.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 12, 'A branch named ''fire'' is typed with a probability above one. What does the engine say?', '["tree.branches: the branch probabilities sum to more than 1, and the branch named ''fire'' has been capped at 1 to close the set", "tree.branches[0].frequencyPerYr: ''fire'' must have a frequency of 0 or more per year", "tree.branches[0].probability: ''fire'' must be a probability in [0, 1]", "tree.branches: every branch set must be exhaustive and exclusive"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those are the engine''s own words, from the refusal table: the field is `tree.branches[0].probability` and the message is that ''fire'' must be a probability in [0, 1]. The engine caps no input. A branch carries a probability, so no branch field is a frequency per year, and the bare line about exhaustive and exclusive sets is only the tail of the sum refusal.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 13, 'An event tree is called with an initiating frequency of zero. What happens?', '["It runs and returns every leaf at a frequency of zero, since a tree with a zero root is valid arithmetic that simply gives no events.", "It runs with an initiating frequency of 1 per year in its place, so that the leaves read as conditional probabilities.", "It is refused on `tree.branches`, because a zero root leaves no branch set with anything to share out between its branches.", "It is refused: initiatingFrequencyPerYr: must be a frequency above 0 per year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The refusal table gives the engine''s own words for a missing or zero root: initiatingFrequencyPerYr: must be a frequency above 0 per year. The units table asks for a root above 0, although a single scenario may carry a frequency of 0. The engine substitutes no value, and the field it names is the initiating frequency itself.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 14, 'Why must every branch set in an event tree be exhaustive and exclusive?', '["So that the branch probabilities sum to one and the leaves share out exactly the frequency that reached the node.", "So that each leaf frequency comes out below its branch probability, which lets a reviewer tell frequencies and probabilities apart.", "So that the engine can take the last branch of every set as one minus the others and save the analyst typing it in.", "So that two leaves never share an outcome name, which would pool them into a single total."]'::jsonb, 0, 'An exhaustive and exclusive set covers every case once, so its probabilities sum to one and the leaves below a node share out exactly the frequency that reached it; that is why the overfill leaves sum back to 0.002000000000 per year. The engine computes no branch for the analyst, and leaves may share an outcome: the two pool fire leaves pool by design.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-event-trees', 15, 'In the overfill tree, what frequency does the engine give the outcome ''no release''?', '["0.002000000000 per year, the whole initiating frequency.", "0.001900000000 per year, the leaf where the high level alarm is answered.", "0.000085500000 per year, the ''no fire'' total.", "0.000063000000 per year, the leaf in which the spill stays in the bund and is never ignited."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The alarm answered path has a probability of 0.950000000000, so the ''no release'' leaf is 0.001900000000 per year. The whole 0.002000000000 is the sum of every leaf, ''no release'' included. 0.000085500000 is the separate ''no fire'' total, and 0.000063000000 is the single leaf where the spill stays in the bund and is never ignited.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 1, 'A continuous flammable release goes through `flammableReleaseEventTree`. Which four outcomes come back?', '["Jet fire, pool fire, flash fire and explosion, since every release that reaches the tree is taken to ignite in the end.", "Immediate ignition, delayed ignition and no ignition, with the flash fire and the explosion left for the analyst to split.", "Fire, toxic cloud, explosion and safe dispersal, the last taken as a stated input from the consequence course.", "Jet or pool fire, flash fire, explosion and no ignition."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest names the four: immediate ignition gives a jet or pool fire; without it, delayed ignition gives a vapour cloud that burns as a flash fire or explodes; without either, nothing ignites. Jet and pool fire are one outcome here and ''no ignition'' is always a leaf. The engine does the split itself with the preset or a stated one, and a toxic cloud is no outcome of this tree.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 2, 'EREMOR''s gas release runs at 5e-4 per year with immediate ignition 0.1, delayed ignition 0.3 given no immediate ignition, and the preset split. What explosion frequency does the engine return?', '["0.000060000000 per year, the figure a tree gives when the delayed ignition probability is taken as unconditional", "0.000054000000", "0.000081000000 per year, the figure a tree gives when the split is swapped to 0.4 flash fire and 0.6 explosion", "0.000135000000 per year, the figure a tree gives when every delayed ignition is counted as an explosion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine multiplies 5e-4 by 0.9 for no immediate ignition, by 0.3 for delayed ignition and by 0.4 for the explosion share, giving 0.000054000000 per year. Taking delayed ignition as unconditional gives 0.000060000000, swapping the split gives 0.000081000000, and counting every delayed ignition as an explosion gives 0.000135000000. Each is a real tree that closes, so the engine runs it without complaint.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 3, 'On the same EREMOR release, what flash fire frequency does the engine return?', '["0.000081000000 per year, on the path no immediate ignition then delayed ignition then flash fire.", "0.000054000000 per year, since the flash fire takes the 0.4 share of every delayed ignition and the explosion takes the 0.6.", "0.000050000000 per year, the fire that follows an immediate ignition.", "0.000135000000 per year, every delayed ignition burning as a flash fire."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The flash fire path is no immediate ignition then delayed ignition then flash fire, at 0.000081000000 per year with the preset share of 0.6. 0.000054000000 is the explosion, which takes the 0.4 share, and reading them the other way round swaps the split. 0.000050000000 is the jet or pool fire from immediate ignition, and 0.000135000000 is every delayed ignition before the split.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 4, 'How often per year does the EREMOR release end with nothing ignited at all?', '["0.000054000000 per year, the release that ignites late and explodes.", "0.000050000000 per year, the release that ignites at once, since the tree reads each probability as the chance of not igniting.", "0.000315000000, the release that escapes both immediate and delayed ignition.", "0.000135000000 per year, the release that ignites late."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine returns 0.000315000000 per year for no immediate ignition then no ignition: of the release that escapes immediate ignition, only the share that also escapes delayed ignition is left with no fire at all. The part that ignites late burns as a flash fire or explodes, so it is never ''no ignition'': 0.000135000000 is every delayed ignition and 0.000054000000 its explosion share. 0.000050000000 is the jet or pool fire from immediate ignition.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 5, 'What does the engine do with the delayed ignition probability that it is given?', '["It treats it as the probability of delayed ignition for the whole release, and adds it to the immediate ignition probability.", "It treats it as CONDITIONAL on no immediate ignition, and multiplies it by one minus the immediate ignition probability.", "It treats it as unconditional and multiplies it straight into the initiating frequency, which is what the 0.000060000000 figure is.", "It treats it as the flash fire share of the vapour cloud, and gives the explosion one minus that share of it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest says the delayed ignition probability is CONDITIONAL on no immediate ignition, and the engine multiplies it by one minus the immediate ignition probability. Taking it as unconditional is exactly the wrong build that gives 0.000060000000 per year for the explosion. Adding it to the immediate probability double counts the release, and the flash fire share is a separate input, the split.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 6, 'What split does the engine use between flash fire and explosion when the caller gives none, and what does its basis say?', '["Flash fire 0.4 and explosion 0.6, and the basis says \"explosion 0.6, flash fire 0.4 (TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999) section 4.8)\".", "Flash fire 0.5 and explosion 0.5, with the basis saying the split was set even because no split was given by the caller.", "None: the engine refuses the call until the caller states a split for the vapour cloud.", "Flash fire 0.6 and explosion 0.4, and the basis says \"flash fire 0.6, explosion 0.4 (TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999) section 4.8)\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The preset split is flash fire 0.6 and explosion 0.4, and the basis quotes it verbatim with its source, Purple Book section 4.8. Swapping it to 0.4 flash fire and 0.6 explosion is one of the wrong builds, and it moves the explosion frequency by a factor of 1.500000. The engine sets no even split of its own, and with the preset available it runs the call.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 7, 'An analyst states the split as { flashFire: 0.6, explosion: 0.4 } for the EREMOR release. What changes in the result?', '["Only the basis, which now says \"as given\"; the four frequencies are the same to the last bit.", "The four frequencies, which move in the last decimal because a stated split is rounded differently from the preset one.", "Nothing at all, since the engine ignores a stated split whenever it matches the preset split to the last digit.", "The explosion frequency, which is recomputed as unconditional once a split is stated by hand."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest says stating the split as { flashFire: 0.6, explosion: 0.4 } gives the same four frequencies to the last bit, and that the basis then says "as given". The engine records what it was given, so the basis does change. Stating a split has no bearing on how delayed ignition is treated; it stays conditional on no immediate ignition.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 8, 'The EREMOR explosion frequency is built with the split swapped, 0.4 flash fire and 0.6 explosion. By what factor does the result sit above the engine''s own?', '["1.111111, giving 0.000060000000 per year in place of 0.000054000000 for the same explosion outcome of the tree.", "2.500000, giving 0.000135000000 per year in place of 0.000054000000 for the same explosion outcome of the tree.", "1.500000, giving 0.000081000000 per year in place of 0.000054000000.", "1.000000, since the engine refuses a swapped split and returns its own figure instead."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest''s table puts the swapped split at 0.000081000000 per year, 1.500000 times the right one. 1.111111 is the factor for delayed ignition taken as unconditional, and 2.500000 the factor for every delayed ignition counted as an explosion. A swapped split closes, so the engine runs it without complaint and cannot know the numbers were typed the wrong way round.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 9, 'Why does the engine run a wrongly built tree, such as one with delayed ignition taken as unconditional, without complaint?', '["A tree that closes is valid arithmetic whatever it means; the engine cannot know that a branch belongs under another, and the analyst draws the tree.", "It checks every tree against the Purple Book structure and runs the wrong one only because the sum of its branches happens to match.", "It does complain: a basis note is added whenever an explosion frequency sits above the flash fire frequency of the same tree.", "It refuses any delayed ignition that is not typed as conditional."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest says each wrong build is a real tree and the engine runs it without complaint: a tree that closes is valid arithmetic whatever it means, and the engine cannot know that a branch belongs under another. There is no structural check against a source and no basis note about the order of two frequencies, and the engine cannot tell how an analyst meant a probability.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 10, 'A continuous release of exactly 10 kg/s of a gas of average to high reactivity is looked up in Purple Book Table 4.5. What does the engine return?', '["0.2, in the small band, because the printed band edge of 10 kg/s belongs to the band that sits below it in the table.", "0.7, in the large band, because a release at a band edge is always placed in the larger band on the side of safety.", "It refuses, since 10 kg/s is an edge that the printed table does not say how to place.", "0.5, in the medium band, because the engine reads the printed middle band as closed at both ends."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The printed middle band is "10 - 100 kg/s" and the engine reads it as CLOSED at both ends, so exactly 10 kg/s is medium, 0.5 for this class; the basis says "table lookup; the middle band is closed at both ends". 0.2 is the small band cell at 5 kg/s and 0.7 the large band at 200 kg/s. The engine answers at the edge with its declared reading.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 11, 'In the engine''s lookups of Table 4.5, what does the k1-liquid column give across every release size?', '["0.02 for a small release, 0.04 for a medium one and 0.09 for a large one, rising with the release size.", "0.065 at every size, in the small, medium or large band that the size falls in.", "0.2 for a small release, 0.5 for a medium one and 0.7 for a large one, the same values as the reactive gases.", "No value: a liquid has no row in a table that classifies gases."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s lookups print 0.065 for k1-liquid at every continuous rate and every instantaneous mass, each in its own band. 0.02, 0.04 and 0.09 are the gas-low-reactivity column, and 0.2, 0.5 and 0.7 the gas-average-high-reactivity column. k1-liquid is one of the three substance classes the engine accepts.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 12, 'What does the digest say the direct ignition table and the 0.6 and 0.4 split rest on, and what follows for a capstone?', '["A second independent reading made by the oracle, so a capstone may ask for any cell of the table to be looked up and graded.", "The golden records, which print every cell with the answer beside it, so each capstone grades the lookup against the golden.", "A single transcription of one source, read once and never checked against a second reading, so every capstone STATES the ignition probabilities and the split.", "A worked example printed in the source."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says the table cells and the split are transcriptions of one source, read once, and nothing in the engine''s validation checks them against a second reading. So this course teaches them and every capstone states the ignition probabilities and the split it uses rather than asking for a lookup. No second reading exists and the digest cites no worked example of the table.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 13, 'A caller states a vapour cloud split of 0.6 flash fire and 0.3 explosion. Which field does the engine name in its refusal?', '["The field `delayedIgnitionProbability`, since the split sits under the delayed ignition branch of the tree the engine builds.", "No field at all: the engine closes the split by giving the explosion the 0.4 that the preset would have given it.", "The field `tree.branches`, the top of the tree, since a split that fails leaves the whole tree open.", "The field `vapourCloudSplit`, with the failing set named by its path inside the tree the engine builds for the release."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The refusal table names `vapourCloudSplit`, and the engine''s own words are: vapourCloudSplit: tree.branches[1].next.branches[0].next.branches: the branch probabilities sum to 0.8999999999999999, not 1 (tolerance 1e-9): every branch set must be exhaustive and exclusive. The field is the input the caller typed, and the path inside the message points to the set that failed, so it is neither the delayed ignition input nor the top of the tree. The engine alters no input and falls back to no preset once a split is given.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 14, 'A continuous release is sent to `pbDirectIgnitionProbability` with no mass rate. What does the engine say?', '["massRateKgS: a continuous release needs a rate above 0 kg/s", "massKg: an instantaneous release needs a mass above 0 kg, and the call has been read as instantaneous in its place", "releaseType: must be ''continuous'' or ''instantaneous''", "massRateKgS: no rate was given, so the small band has been taken as the most cautious reading of the table"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table, naming `massRateKgS`. The engine does not switch a continuous call to an instantaneous one, and it assumes no band. The release type message is real engine text for a type the table does not have, and here the type was valid.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-a-flammable-release', 15, 'Among the release sizes the digest''s lookup table prints, which does the engine place in the large band for gas-low-reactivity, and with what probability?', '["A continuous 100 kg/s or an instantaneous 10000 kg, at 0.09, since the top edge of the middle band opens into the large band.", "A continuous 50 kg/s or an instantaneous 5000 kg, at 0.04, the value the column gives to every release of middle size or more.", "A continuous 200 kg/s or an instantaneous 20000 kg, at 0.09.", "A continuous 200 kg/s or an instantaneous 20000 kg, at 0.7."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s lookups put gas-low-reactivity at 0.09 in the large band for a continuous 200 kg/s and an instantaneous 20000 kg. Exactly 100 kg/s and 10000 kg stay in the medium band at 0.04, because the middle band is closed at both ends. 0.7 is the large band value of the average to high reactivity gases.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 1, 'What does location-specific individual risk (LSIR) describe, in the engine''s own model string?', '["LSIR = sum f_i x Pd_i, a person present at the location all the time, outdoors and unprotected", "LSIR = sum f_i x Pd_i x occupancy_i, a named person at the location for the hours on their own roster, indoors when off shift", "LSIR = sum Pd_i, the chance of death at the location summed over the scenarios, a person present all the time and unprotected", "LSIR = max f_i x Pd_i, the worst scenario at the location, a person present all the time, outdoors and unprotected"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s model string, verbatim, is "LSIR = sum f_i x Pd_i, a person present at the location all the time, outdoors and unprotected". Occupancy enters only at the next step, the individual risk per annum of a named person. Summing probabilities of death alone gives a number with no unit, and taking the worst scenario alone drops every other contribution.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 2, 'EREMOR''s four outcomes are run against the process deck, with a probability of death stated for each. What LSIR does the engine return there?', '["0.000081000000 per year, the flash fire contribution taken alone because it is the largest one at the process deck", "0.000154803000 per year, the three LSIRs of the process deck, the control room and the accommodation summed", "0.000148150000", "0.000037800000 per year, the explosion contribution, taken as the process deck''s figure because an explosion reaches furthest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine sums f times Pd over the four outcomes and returns 0.000148150000 per year at the process deck. Taking only the largest contribution, the flash fire, gives 0.000081000000 and drops three scenarios. 0.000154803000 adds in two other places, which describes no single location, and 0.000037800000 is the explosion''s share alone.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 3, 'What is the LSIR at the EREMOR control room, and which scenario carries most of it?', '["0.000006545000 per year, with the flash fire carrying 0.546743 of it, just as the flash fire leads at the process deck.", "0.000006545000 per year, with the explosion carrying 0.825057 of it.", "0.000000108000 per year, with the explosion carrying 1.000000 of it.", "0.000148150000 per year, the same as the process deck."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns 0.000006545000 per year at the control room, with the explosion as the largest contribution at 0.825057. The flash fire carries 0.546743 at the process deck, and at the control room its probability of death is zero. 0.000000108000 with the explosion at 1.000000 is the accommodation. An LSIR belongs to one place, so the process deck figure says nothing about the control room.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 4, 'The EREMOR flash fire has a frequency of 0.000081000000 per year. What does it add to the LSIR at the control room, and why?', '["0.000081000000 per year, since every scenario adds its whole frequency to the LSIR at a place however far the place lies from it.", "0.000006545000 per year, the whole control room LSIR, since the flash fire is the outcome that sets the LSIR at every place.", "0.000081000000 per year times the occupancy of the control room, which is where a place''s distance enters the sum.", "0.000000000000 per year, because its stated probability of death there is zero: the control room lies outside the flammable cloud."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A scenario whose probability of death is zero at a place contributes zero there, and the digest says the flash fire at the control room, which lies outside the flammable cloud, adds 0.000000000000. A frequency adds only through its probability of death, which is a stated input. 0.000006545000 is the whole control room LSIR, which comes from the other three outcomes, and occupancy belongs to the individual risk per annum of a person.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 5, 'What share of the EREMOR process deck LSIR does the flash fire carry, and what is its contribution?', '["0.255147, a contribution of 0.000037800000 per year.", "0.168748, a contribution of 0.000025000000 per year.", "1.000000 of it, since a probability of death of 1 at the process deck means the flash fire is all there is to the LSIR.", "0.546743 of it, a contribution of 0.000081000000 per year."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The flash fire has a stated probability of death of 1 at the process deck, so its contribution is 0.000081000000 per year, 0.546743 of the LSIR and the largest share. 0.255147 and 0.000037800000 are the explosion''s share and contribution, and 0.168748 and 0.000025000000 the jet or pool fire''s, whatever order the outcomes are listed in. A probability of death of 1 makes the contribution equal the frequency; the other three outcomes still add theirs.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 6, 'At the process deck the explosion has a frequency of 0.000054000000 per year and a stated probability of death of 0.7. What contribution does the engine give it?', '["0.000054000000 per year, since a stated probability of death above one half counts the whole frequency at that place.", "0.000037800000 per year, 0.255147 of the LSIR.", "0.000025000000 per year, 0.168748 of the LSIR, the jet or pool fire''s contribution at the process deck.", "0.000004350000 per year, 0.029362 of the LSIR, which is the pool fire''s contribution at the process deck."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The contribution is f times Pd, 0.000054000000 times 0.7, which the engine returns as 0.000037800000 per year, 0.255147 of the process deck LSIR. No threshold turns a probability of death into a whole count. 0.000025000000 and 0.168748 belong to the jet or pool fire, and 0.000004350000 and 0.029362 to the pool fire.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 7, 'Which EREMOR scenario gives the smallest contribution to the process deck LSIR?', '["The pool fire, at 0.000004350000 per year, 0.029362 of the LSIR.", "The jet or pool fire, at 0.000025000000 per year.", "The explosion, at 0.000037800000 per year, since its stated probability of death is lower than the flash fire''s at the deck.", "The flash fire, since its probability of death is taken as zero at the process deck once the cloud has burnt."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pooled pool fire outcome, 0.000014500000 per year with a stated probability of death of 0.3, gives 0.000004350000 per year, 0.029362 of the LSIR, the smallest of the four. The jet or pool fire gives 0.000025000000 and the explosion 0.000037800000. The flash fire''s probability of death at the process deck is stated as 1, so it gives the largest contribution.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 8, 'What LSIR does the engine return at the EREMOR accommodation, and why does one scenario carry all of it?', '["0.000006545000 per year; the accommodation shares the control room''s LSIR, since both sit away from the process deck on the platform.", "0.000000000000 per year; an accommodation block is indoors, so every probability of death there is taken as zero by the engine.", "0.000000108000 per year; the explosion is the only outcome with a probability of death above zero there, so it carries 1.000000.", "0.000000108000 per year; the flash fire carries all of it, since a vapour cloud drifts further than a blast."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At the accommodation only the explosion has a stated probability of death above zero, 0.002, so the LSIR is 0.000000108000 per year and the explosion carries 1.000000 of it. Each place has its own LSIR, so the control room''s 0.000006545000 is no guide. The engine sets no probability of death to zero for being indoors; every one is stated, and the flash fire''s is zero there.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 9, 'An analyst adds up the four stated probabilities of death at the process deck without their frequencies. What has been produced?', '["The LSIR per year, since each probability of death already carries its scenario frequency folded inside it as a stated input.", "A number with no unit at all, which is no LSIR; the LSIR is the SUM of f times Pd.", "The probability of death of a person at the process deck over a whole year, which the engine reports beside the LSIR.", "A worst case LSIR, larger than the engine''s figure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest says summing the probabilities of death without their frequencies gives a number with no unit at all, and that the LSIR is the SUM of f times Pd. A probability of death is a conditional probability given the scenario, so it carries no frequency. The engine reports no yearly probability of death beside the LSIR, and a sum with no unit is no bound on an LSIR, however large it looks.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 10, 'Why does an LSIR describe a PLACE rather than a person?', '["It assumes someone present at the location all the time, outdoors and unprotected, so it holds no occupancy of any real person.", "It is computed only for places that are occupied all the time, such as a control room, and is refused for places visited now and then.", "It adds the hours of everyone on the roster at the location together, so that no single person''s hours are carried in the sum.", "It is the LSIR of the most exposed person on site, standing at the place where the probabilities of death are highest."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The model string says a person present at the location all the time, outdoors and unprotected, so the LSIR is a property of the place and carries no occupancy. The engine computes it for any place given its scenarios, occupied or not. Hours enter only when one person''s individual risk per annum is built over the places they occupy.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 11, 'Purple Book Appendix 6.B works one individual risk contribution at one grid point. Where does this course start its steps, and why?', '["At the release frequency, running every step of the chain in this course so that each learner reproduces the source from the start.", "At the contribution per year, taking every earlier value from the source and reproducing none of the chain from the printed numbers.", "At the wind sector count, since that is the first step the source prints.", "At the centreline probability of death, taken as given, because the concentration, the probit and the probability integral before it are consequence modelling and belong to the consequence course."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest says the early steps, the concentration, the probit and the probability integral, are consequence modelling and belong to the consequence course; from the effective cloud width onward the steps are QRA arithmetic, and this course starts there. The earlier steps are never run here, and the course does reproduce the later ones, from the probability of death through to the contribution.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 12, 'The whole Appendix 6.B chain, as the golden records it, gives a probability of death of 0.380294556093, and the source prints 0.381. What explains the gap?', '["The engine has a defect in its coverage probability, which the printed 0.381 exposes at the third decimal of the chain.", "The source uses 8766 hours a year where the engine uses 8760, which moves every probability of death at the third decimal.", "The printed 0.381 follows only from the rounded effective cloud width of 86.2 m; the source is internally rounded, and both routes reproduce its contribution of 7e-9 per year.", "The engine rounds each step to three decimals and so loses the last digit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says the whole chain gives 0.380294556093, which rounds to 0.380, and that the printed 0.381 follows only from the rounded effective cloud width of 86.2 m. The source is internally rounded, which is a fact about the SOURCE, and both routes reproduce its printed contribution of 7e-9 per year to two significant figures. No hours enter this chain, and the engine rounds no step.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 13, 'Step 6 of Appendix 6.B is run through `locationIndividualRisk` with f PM Pphi of 1.84e-8 per year and the printed probability of death of 0.381. What does the engine return?', '["0.000000007010 per year, which the source prints as 7e-9.", "0.000000006997 per year, since the engine replaces the printed 0.381 with its own whole chain value before it multiplies.", "0.000000000000 per year, since a contribution that small falls below the 1e-8 contour and is dropped as negligible by the engine.", "1.84e-8 per year, since the probability of death has already been folded into the frequency given for the step."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Through `locationIndividualRisk` the product 1.84e-8 times 0.381 is 0.000000007010 per year, and the source prints 7e-9. 0.000000006997 is the whole chain''s contribution, which uses the engine''s own probability of death; this call uses the printed one as stated. The engine drops no contribution for being small, and the probability of death still multiplies the frequency.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 14, 'What inputs does the digest give for the Appendix 6.B chain, and how is the coverage probability formed?', '["A frequency f of 5e-7 per year, 12 wind sectors and R = 361 m; the coverage probability is ECW / R, with the sector count used later.", "A frequency f of 1.84e-8 per year, 12 wind sectors and R = 86.2 m; the coverage probability is nws ECW / (2 pi R).", "A frequency f of 5e-7 per year, 12 wind sectors and R = 361 m; the coverage probability is Pci = nws ECW / (2 pi R).", "A frequency of 7e-9 per year and a probability of death of 0.835, with no coverage step at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The golden inputs are a loss of containment frequency f of 5e-7 per year, 12 wind sectors and R = 361 m, and the coverage probability is Pci = nws ECW / (2 pi R). 1.84e-8 per year is f PM Pphi, a later product, and 86.2 m is the printed effective cloud width. 7e-9 is the printed contribution and 0.835 the centreline probability of death, and the coverage step sits between them.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-location-specific-individual-risk', 15, 'An LSIR call names a scenario ''fire'' with a frequency below zero. What does the engine return?', '["scenarios[0].frequencyPerYr: ''fire'' must be a frequency above 0 per year, since a scenario that never happens has no place in the LSIR", "An LSIR with that scenario''s contribution set to zero, since a frequency below zero is read as a scenario that cannot happen", "scenarios: must be a non-empty list of scenarios", "scenarios[0].frequencyPerYr: ''fire'' must have a frequency of 0 or more per year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table. A scenario may carry a frequency of 0 or more, so zero is allowed and only a negative value is refused; the words about a frequency above 0 belong to an event tree root. The engine alters no input to zero, and the empty list message is real engine text for a call with no scenarios.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 1, 'Which formula does the basis print for one person''s IRPA?', '["IRPA = sum LSIR_j, the LSIRs of every place a person visits added with no weighting", "IRPA = max LSIR_j x occupancy_j, the single place where the person carries the most individual risk over the year", "IRPA = sum LSIR_j x occupancy_j x v_j (v as supplied, default 1)", "IRPA = sum f_i x Pd_i x occupancy, a single occupancy for the whole platform"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine''s model string, verbatim, is "IRPA = sum LSIR_j x occupancy_j x v_j (v as supplied, default 1)". Adding the LSIRs with no occupancy treats one person as present everywhere all year. Taking the largest place alone drops the others, and the occupancy belongs to each place in turn, since a person is in one place at a time.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 2, 'The EREMOR operator spends 1000 hours a year on the process deck, 800 in the control room and 2560 in the accommodation (stated). What IRPA does the engine return?', '["0.000154803000 per year, the three LSIRs of the places added together with no occupancy applied", "0.000017529373 per year, with every hour count divided by 8766 hours to make the fraction", "0.000016912100 per year, the process deck alone", "0.000017541379"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine converts each hour count at 8760 hours a year, multiplies each LSIR by its fraction and sums, giving 0.000017541379 per year. Summing the LSIRs with no occupancy gives 0.000154803000, dividing by 8766 gives 0.000017529373, and the process deck alone gives 0.000016912100. Each is a wrong method the digest measures.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 3, 'What occupancy fraction does the engine give the operator''s 1000 hours on the process deck?', '["0.091324200913, the control room''s 800 hours.", "0.114155251142, which is 1000 hours over 8760.", "0.292237442922, from the 2560 hours.", "0.497716894977, the operator''s whole year across the three places."]'::jsonb, 1, '1000 hours over 8760 is an occupancy of 0.114155251142. 0.091324200913 is the control room''s 800 hours and 0.292237442922 the accommodation''s 2560 hours. 0.497716894977 is the total occupancy over all three places, which is no fraction of any single one.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 4, 'What total occupancy does the EREMOR operator carry over the year, and what does it say?', '["0.497716894977 of the year; the rest of the year the operator is away from all three places.", "1.000000000000 of the year, the whole year.", "0.114155251142 of the year, the process deck alone.", "The operator''s hours in total, left unconverted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine reports a total occupancy of 0.497716894977 of the year for the operator, so the rest of the year is spent away from these places, where this assessment adds nothing. A roster need not fill the year; the check only stops it going over. 0.114155251142 is the process deck share alone, and the engine reports fractions of the year.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 5, 'A supervisor is rostered by fraction of the year (0.05, 0.2 and 0.25 across deck, control room and quarters, stated). Which IRPA comes back?', '["0.000017541379 per year, the same as the operator''s, since both people work on the same three places of the platform", "0.000154803000 per year, since fractions of the year are added to the LSIRs rather than multiplied into them by the engine", "0.000016912100 per year, the operator''s process deck share carried over", "0.000008743500"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The supervisor''s IRPA is 0.000008743500 per year, the sum of each LSIR times the stated fraction. Individual risk per annum belongs to one person, so the operator''s 0.000017541379 on the same places does not carry over. 0.000154803000 is the LSIRs summed with no occupancy. 0.000016912100 is the operator''s process deck contribution, which belongs to the operator''s hours and drops two places as well.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 6, 'In the operator''s IRPA, what does the control room add, and how?', '["0.000006545000 per year, the whole control room LSIR, since the operator is in the control room on every shift worked.", "0.000016912100 per year, which is the figure the engine gives the process deck for the operator''s hours.", "0.000000597717 per year, its LSIR of 0.000006545000 times an occupancy of 0.091324200913.", "0.000000031562 per year, the accommodation''s share of the operator''s IRPA."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The control room contribution is its LSIR, 0.000006545000 per year, times the operator''s occupancy there, 0.091324200913, which the engine returns as 0.000000597717 per year. The whole LSIR would mean presence all year. 0.000016912100 is the process deck contribution and 0.000000031562 the accommodation''s.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 7, 'A person is in one place at a time. How does the engine hold a roster to that?', '["The fractions may not exceed one at any place or in total, and the check lets the total reach one exactly.", "Each fraction may not exceed one, and the total across places is left unchecked so long as each place is inside 0 to 1.", "The total must equal one exactly, so a roster that leaves part of the year unaccounted for is refused by the engine.", "The total may reach 1.1, a margin for overtime."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest says the fractions may not exceed one at any place or in total, and that the occupancy check allows the total to reach one exactly. A roster of 0.6 and 0.5 is refused although each is below one, so the total is checked. A roster need not fill the year; the operator''s total is 0.497716894977. The engine allows no margin over the whole year.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 8, 'A roster gives ''deck'' more hours than a year holds. What does the engine say, in its own words?', '["locations: the occupancy fractions sum to more than one", "locations[0].hoursPerYr: ''deck'' must lie in [0, 8760] hours", "The hours capped at 8760 and the IRPA formed from them", "locations[0].hoursPerYr: must be above 0 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table for more hours than a year holds. The total occupancy message belongs to a roster whose fractions sum above one, and here the fault is one place''s hours. The engine caps no input, the field named is the hours the analyst typed, and a zero hour count is allowed, since the range runs from 0.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 9, 'The analyst applies a vulnerability factor of 0.5 in the accommodation (stated). What operator IRPA does the engine return?', '["Half of the operator''s IRPA, since the engine applies the factor to every place the operator occupies", "0.000017541379 per year, unchanged, since the engine ignores a vulnerability factor for individual risk and reports the default", "0.000017525598", "0.000016912100 per year, the process deck alone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With 0.5 applied in the accommodation only, the operator IRPA is 0.000017525598 per year: the accommodation contribution halves and nothing else moves. The factor applies at the place it is supplied for, so it never halves the whole IRPA. The engine uses a supplied factor and returns it beside that place''s contribution; 0.000017541379 is the figure with the default of 1.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 10, 'Why is the vulnerability factor 1 by default, and whose is it?', '["It is the analyst''s own: no source the engine read gives a vulnerability factor for individual risk, so the default is 1.", "It is the Purple Book''s: the indoor and outdoor fractions set it, and the engine takes the indoor figure for any building.", "It is the engine''s own estimate: it lowers the factor for any place with a stated probability of death below one half.", "It is fixed at 1 and cannot be supplied."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest says the vulnerability factor is the analyst''s own, that no source the engine read gives one for individual risk, and so the default is 1 and the result returns the factor used at each place. The Purple Book indoor and outdoor fractions are for societal risk, and they are no vulnerability factor. The engine estimates nothing, and a factor can be supplied, as the accommodation example shows.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 11, 'A vulnerability factor above one is supplied for ''deck''. What does the engine return?', '["An IRPA in which the deck contribution is raised by half, since a factor above one marks a place that is more dangerous than its LSIR says", "The factor capped at 1, with a note in the basis", "locations[0].occupancyFraction: ''deck'': give exactly one of occupancyFraction and hoursPerYr", "locations[0].vulnerabilityFactor: ''deck'' must be a factor in [0, 1]"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table for a vulnerability factor above one. A factor may only reduce a contribution, so no IRPA is formed from it, and the engine caps no input. The message about giving exactly one of the fraction and the hours is real engine text for a different fault.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 12, 'Why is dividing the operator''s hours by 8766 a wrong method here, and what does it give?', '["The engine converts hours at 8766 to allow for leap years, so dividing by 8766 is right and gives the engine''s 0.000017541379.", "The engine converts hours at 8760, the H3 HOURS_PER_YEAR; 8766 gives 0.000017529373 per year in place of 0.000017541379.", "The engine takes hours with no conversion, so any divisor is wrong, and dividing by 8766 gives 0.000154803000 per year.", "It gives 0.000016912100 per year, the process deck alone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hours convert at 8760 hours a year, the H3 engine''s HOURS_PER_YEAR, and the digest measures the 8766 route at 0.000017529373 per year against the engine''s 0.000017541379. 0.000154803000 is the no occupancy sum and 0.000016912100 the process deck alone, which are two other wrong methods.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 13, 'How much of the operator''s yearly figure comes from the hours in the accommodation block?', '["0.000000108000 per year, the whole accommodation LSIR.", "0.000000597717 per year, the control room share.", "0.000000031562 per year, from 2560 hours at an occupancy of 0.292237442922.", "Nothing, since off shift carries no individual risk."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The accommodation LSIR of 0.000000108000 per year times the occupancy of 0.292237442922 from 2560 hours gives 0.000000031562 per year. The whole LSIR would mean presence all year. 0.000000597717 is the control room contribution. Time off shift still counts wherever the person is, so the accommodation adds its share.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 14, 'What does an IRPA add to the LSIRs of the places a person visits?', '["How long that one person spends at each place, as a fraction of the year, with any vulnerability factor the analyst supplies.", "The probability of death of that person at each place, worked out by the engine from how far each place sits from the release.", "The number of people at each place, so that the IRPA counts every death at once.", "A new set of scenario frequencies for each place, raised to allow for the extra time the person spends there."]'::jsonb, 0, 'An IRPA weights each LSIR by the fraction of the year one person spends there, times any vulnerability factor the analyst supplies. Probabilities of death are already inside each LSIR as stated inputs from the consequence course. Counting how many die at once is societal risk and the next tier''s question, and the scenario frequencies are the same ones the LSIRs used.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-individual-risk-per-annum', 15, 'The process deck LSIR is 0.000148150000 per year, yet the operator''s IRPA is far smaller. Why?', '["The engine applies a vulnerability factor of 0.5 to every place by default, which halves each LSIR before it is summed into the IRPA.", "The IRPA takes the smallest LSIR among the places the operator visits, since that is where the operator spends most of the year.", "The IRPA divides the three LSIRs by the number of places visited.", "The operator spends 0.114155251142 of the year on the deck, so the deck adds 0.000016912100 per year to the IRPA."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The deck LSIR assumes presence all the time; the operator is there for 0.114155251142 of the year, so the deck contributes 0.000016912100 per year and the whole IRPA is 0.000017541379. The vulnerability factor defaults to 1 and is the analyst''s own. The IRPA weights every place by its occupancy, so it neither picks the smallest LSIR nor averages the places.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 1, 'Along the EREMOR transect the jet fire runs at 5e-5 per year and the explosion at 2e-5 per year (stated), with probabilities of death of 0.35 and 0.4 stated at 100 m. What LSIR does the engine return at 100 m?', '["0.000061000000 per year, which is the LSIR the engine returns at 50 m, nearer the release on the same transect", "0.000025500000", "0.000004500000 per year, the figure at 150 m", "0.000017500000 per year, the jet fire contribution alone, since only the largest scenario sets the LSIR at a distance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 100 m the engine sums f times the probability of death over both scenarios, 5e-5 times 0.35 plus 2e-5 times 0.4, and returns 0.000025500000 per year. 0.000061000000 is the LSIR at 50 m and 0.000004500000 at 150 m. The jet fire''s share alone drops the explosion, and the LSIR is always the sum over scenarios.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 2, 'On the same transect, why is the LSIR at 0 m exactly 0.000070000000 per year?', '["Both stated probabilities of death are 1 there, so the LSIR is the two frequencies added: 5e-5 plus 2e-5 per year.", "The engine caps the LSIR at the release point at the largest contour it knows, and 0.000070000000 is that cap.", "It is the jet fire frequency alone, since an explosion has no effect at its own centre.", "It is the 50 m figure of 0.000061000000 carried back to the release, since the engine sets no LSIR at a distance of zero from the release point itself."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At 0 m both stated probabilities of death are 1, so each scenario adds its whole frequency and the LSIR is 5e-5 plus 2e-5, 0.000070000000 per year. The engine caps nothing, and the 1e-4 contour sits above this figure. The explosion''s probability of death at 0 m is stated as 1, and the engine computes the LSIR at 0 m like any other distance.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 3, 'Where does the EREMOR transect cross the 1e-5 per year contour?', '["At 150 m, the first printed distance whose LSIR lies below the level, since the engine reports only the distances it was given.", "At 187.323816 m, which is where the transect crosses the 1e-6 contour.", "Midway between 100 m and 150 m, since a crossing is taken at the midpoint of the two bracketing points on the transect.", "At 126.982958 m, interpolated in log10 of the LSIR between the points at 100 m and 150 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The LSIR is 0.000025500000 at 100 m and 0.000004500000 at 150 m, so the level lies between them, and the engine interpolates in log10 of the LSIR to put the crossing at 126.982958 m. It interpolates, so it does not snap to a printed distance or to the midpoint. 187.323816 m is the 1e-6 crossing.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 4, 'The highest level the Purple Book asks for is 1e-4 per year. What crossing does the engine report for it on the EREMOR line?', '["At 0 m, where every transect starts on its highest contour.", "At 126.982958 m, the 1e-5 crossing.", "Nowhere: the LSIR peaks at 0.000070000000 per year at 0 m, below 1e-4.", "At 50 m, the point nearest to 1e-4."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest''s table says none on this transect: the largest LSIR is 0.000070000000 per year at 0 m, below 1e-4, so no crossing exists. The engine reports only real crossings, never a starting point or a nearest point. 126.982958 m is the 1e-5 crossing alone.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 5, 'The 1e-8 crossing sits at exactly 350.000000 m. What rule puts it there?', '["The LSIR is 0.000000020000 at 300 m and zero at 400 m, and with one side zero the crossing is interpolated linearly.", "The engine rounds every crossing to the nearest 50 m.", "It interpolates in log10 of the LSIR as it does everywhere, and the log of zero at 400 m pulls the crossing to the midpoint.", "It takes the last distance with an LSIR above zero, 300 m, and adds half the spacing between the printed distances after it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The basis says the crossing is interpolated in log10 of the LSIR between bracketing points, and linearly in the LSIR when one side is zero. Between 0.000000020000 at 300 m and 0.000000000000 at 400 m the linear rule gives 350.000000 m. The log of zero is undefined, which is why the engine switches rule. It rounds no crossing and adds no spacing.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 6, 'What status does a contour crossing distance have in this course, and why?', '["It is graded at six decimals, since the Purple Book fixes the log10 interpolation between points in its section on contours.", "It is graded only for the 1e-5 contour, which is the one the Purple Book asks for on every plot plan it describes.", "It is reported only as a band, never as a distance.", "It is never graded: the log10 interpolation is a presentation rule the engine chose, and nothing published fixes it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest says the interpolation is a PRESENTATION rule the engine chose; nothing published fixes it, so a crossing distance is never graded. The Purple Book asks for the contours to be shown and prescribes no interpolation, and it asks for five levels rather than one. The engine reports each crossing as a distance in metres.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 7, 'Which contour levels does the engine export as PB_IR_CONTOURS_PER_YR, the ones the Purple Book asks to be shown?', '["1e-5 and 1e-6 per year only, the two levels a plot plan must show whatever else it carries.", "1e-4, 1e-5, 1e-6, 1e-7 and 1e-8 per year.", "1e-4, 1e-5 and 1e-6 per year, the three levels that sit inside the range an LSIR at a place usually takes.", "1e-4 to 1e-8 in steps of 5."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The constants table gives PB_IR_CONTOURS_PER_YR as 1e-4, 1e-5, 1e-6, 1e-7 and 1e-8 per year, and the Purple Book asks for those contours to be shown. Two or three levels are too few, and no set runs in steps of 5: the engine''s contour set is the five decades from 1e-4 down to 1e-8.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 8, 'The digest runs eight golden cases through the engine. What do they rest on?', '["Each reproduces a worked example printed in the Purple Book, one case for each figure of its chapter on event trees.", "Each is a capstone facility, reused as a golden record.", "All are ORACLE-DERIVED: no published source prints an event tree or an IRPA worked example.", "Each was typed from a textbook by the engine''s authors, then checked by the oracle against the same textbook."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says every golden case in that table is ORACLE-DERIVED, and that no published source prints an event tree or an IRPA worked example. They are no reproduction of a printed example, no capstone and no textbook transcription: they were written by the engine''s independent oracle.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 9, 'The golden case flammable-default is run through `eventTree`. What explosion frequency does the engine return?', '["0.000240000000 per year, the figure the case returns for the flash fire outcome", "0.000160000000", "0.000200000000 per year, which is the jet or pool fire outcome of the same golden case", "0.000054000000 per year, the EREMOR explosion carried over"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine returns jet or pool fire 0.000200000000, flash fire 0.000240000000, explosion 0.000160000000 and no ignition 0.000400000000 per year for flammable-default. 0.000240000000 and 0.000200000000 are its flash fire and jet or pool fire. 0.000054000000 belongs to the EREMOR release, a different set of inputs.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 10, 'The golden case three-way-with-pooled-outcome returns three outcomes. What does the engine give the pooled ''fire'' outcome?', '["0.011752000000 per year, the figure the case returns for its ''safe'' outcome", "0.000390000000 per year, which is the ''escalation'' outcome of the same case", "0.000160000000 per year, from another case", "0.000858000000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns fire 0.000858000000, safe 0.011752000000 and escalation 0.000390000000 per year for this case. The ''fire'' total pools every leaf that carries that outcome, as the two pool fire leaves of the overfill tree pool. The other two figures belong to the case''s other outcomes, and 0.000160000000 is the explosion of the flammable-default case.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 11, 'Which IRPA and occupancy come back for the full-occupancy golden record?', '["IRPA 0.000022000000 per year at an occupancy of 1.000000000000.", "0.000041195890 per year at 0.236986301370.", "A refusal, since the year is full.", "IRPA 0.000022000000 at an occupancy of 0.497716894977."]'::jsonb, 0, 'For full-occupancy the engine returns IRPA 0.000022000000 per year at an occupancy of 1.000000000000: the occupancy check allows the total to reach one exactly. 0.000041195890 at 0.236986301370 is the operator-two-areas case, 0.497716894977 is the EREMOR operator''s total, and a total of one exactly is accepted, never refused.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 12, 'The golden case zero-probability-branch holds a branch of probability zero. What does the engine do?', '["It refuses the branch, since a probability of zero means the branch can never be taken and has no place in the tree.", "It removes the branch and its leaf without a message, since a zero leaf adds nothing to any outcome total in the tree.", "It allows it: the branch carries a leaf of frequency zero, and the set still sums to one.", "It raises the branch to 1e-9, the tolerance."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says a branch of probability zero is allowed; it carries a leaf of frequency zero, and the set still sums to one, which is why the case returns a 0.000000000000 and b 0.000400000000. A probability of zero lies inside [0, 1], so nothing is refused, removed or raised.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 13, 'The EREMOR operator carries an IRPA of 0.000017541379 per year. What does that figure NOT tell a reader?', '["What one person carries over a year across the places they occupy.", "The sum of each place''s LSIR times the fraction of the year the operator spends there.", "Which places add most to the operator''s individual risk per annum, since the engine returns each contribution.", "How many people might die at once in one event, which is societal risk and the next tier''s question."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An IRPA follows one person: it says what that person carries over a year, as the sum of each LSIR times the occupancy there, and the engine returns each contribution. It says nothing of how many people might die at once in one event, which is societal risk and belongs to the Professional tier.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 14, 'A capstone asks for one person''s individual risk per annum from a release. In what order does the method run, as this tier teaches it?', '["The IRPA first from the roster, then the LSIR at each place, then the event tree, run backwards to find the release frequency.", "Event tree outcome frequencies, then the LSIR at each place from stated probabilities of death, then the IRPA over the person''s occupancy.", "The direct ignition probability looked up in Table 4.5, then the IRPA straight from the lookup with no LSIR in between.", "The probability of death computed from a probit, then the IRPA; the event tree is a step belonging to the consequence course."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The chain this tier teaches runs forward: the event tree gives each outcome''s frequency, the LSIR at each place sums f times the stated probability of death, and the IRPA weights each LSIR by the person''s occupancy. A capstone states every ignition probability and the split rather than asking for a lookup, and the probability of death is a stated input that belongs to the consequence course.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-one-person-end-to-end', 15, 'An `lsirTransect` call gives two distances but only one probability of death for a scenario named ''fire''. What does the engine say?', '["scenarios[0].fatalityProbabilities: ''fire'' needs one probability of death per distance (2)", "scenarios[0].fatalityProbabilities: the missing probability of death has been taken as zero at the far distance", "distancesM[2]: distances must increase strictly", "scenarios[0].fatalityProbability: ''fire'' must be a probability of death in [0, 1]"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table, and the (2) is the number of distances given. The engine fills in no missing probability of death. The strictly increasing distances message and the [0, 1] probability of death message are real engine text for two other calls.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'An analyst wants the LSIR at a list of distances along one line from a release, and where it passes chosen contour levels. Which function is that?', '["`locationIndividualRisk`, called once for every distance on the line, since it returns contour crossings when it is given more than one place", "`individualRiskPerAnnum`, which takes each distance as a location and returns a crossing wherever the occupancy changes", "`lsirTransect`", "`thermalFatalityTransect`"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The function table gives `lsirTransect` as the call that takes distancesM, scenarios with fatalityProbabilities and contourLevelsPerYr, and returns the LSIR at each distance and where it crosses each level. `locationIndividualRisk` returns one place''s LSIR and no crossings, and `individualRiskPerAnnum` weights places by occupancy. `thermalFatalityTransect` is one of the three functions that run the consequence engine to MAKE a probability of death, which belongs to the consequence course.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'What does `pbDirectIgnitionProbability` need from its caller?', '["releaseType, massRateKgS or massKg, and substance.", "The three inputs of the release tree.", "releaseType and substance only.", "A distance and a wind speed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The function table lists releaseType, massRateKgS or massKg, and substance, and the call returns the Table 4.5 direct ignition probability and its band. The frequency and the two ignition probabilities are the inputs of `flammableReleaseEventTree`. The size is its own input, a rate for a continuous release or a mass for an instantaneous one, and no distance or wind speed enters the table.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'The engine names its sources in every `basis`, from its export `QRA_SOURCES`. What does it cite under the key PB?', '["UK HSE, Reducing risks, protecting people (2001), which the engine cites whenever an individual risk is summed at a place", "UK HSE, Cost Benefit Analysis (CBA) checklist (2003 values), cited for the event tree and the direct ignition table", "The H4 consequence engine, cited as the source of every probability of death it is handed as an input", "TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sources table gives PB as TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999), and the Purple Book is the source the event tree, LSIR and IRPA bases name. R2P2 and the CBA checklist sit under their own keys and are no source of the tree or the table. A probability of death is a stated input, and the engine''s source list carries no key for the consequence engine.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Which of the consequence course''s and the facilities courses'' functions does this engine export?', '["All of them, so that a learner can check a stated probability of death against the consequence course''s own figure.", "None of them: it re-grades nothing those courses own, and the jest suite asserts so.", "Only the flare radiation calls, which belong to the facilities courses.", "Only the plume call, which belongs to the consequence course."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest says the engine re-grades nothing the consequence course or the facilities courses own, exports none of their functions, and that the jest suite asserts so. Flare radiation belongs to the facilities courses and is never used here. A toxic cloud, like a fire or an explosion, is modelled in the consequence course, and this engine takes the probability of death as a stated input.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'What range does the units table allow for an occupancy?', '["0 to 1 as a fraction of the year, or 0 to 8760 hours, and no more than the whole year in total.", "0 to 1 as a fraction at each place, with no limit on the total, since each place is checked on its own.", "Any number of hours up to 8766, a calendar year with leap days, and any fraction up to 1.", "Above 0 only, as for a root frequency."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The units table gives occupancy as a fraction of the year from 0 to 1, or hours from 0 to 8760, and no more than the whole year in total. The total is checked as well as each place, because a person is in one place at a time. The engine converts at 8760 hours a year, and an occupancy of 0 is allowed.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Where does the engine draw its line for a zero frequency: at the start of an event tree, or on a scenario at a place?', '["Both must be above 0 per year.", "A root may be 0 or more; a scenario must be above 0.", "A root must be above 0 per year; a scenario may carry 0 or more per year.", "Both may be any number, negative ones included."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The units table sets the two apart: an event tree''s starting frequency has to be positive, while a scenario handed to the LSIR may sit at zero, as the golden case with-zero-frequency shows. Nothing in the engine accepts a negative frequency, and a scenario at zero simply contributes nothing.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'Someone leaves the name of a branch blank. Quote the refusal.', '["tree.branches[0].name: the branch has been named after its outcome so that its leaf can still be carried into the totals", "tree.branches[0].outcome: every leaf needs an outcome before the tree can be pooled into totals by the engine", "tree.branches: must be a non-empty list of { name, probability, next? }", "tree.branches[0].name: every branch needs a name"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table for a branch with no name. The engine names nothing on the analyst''s behalf. An outcome is optional, since a leaf with no outcome takes its branch name, and the non-empty list message is real engine text for an empty branch set.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'A call to the ignition table gives a release type the table does not have. What does the engine say?', '["releaseType: the release has been read as continuous, the more common of the two types, and looked up in the table", "releaseType: must be ''continuous'' or ''instantaneous''", "massRateKgS: a continuous release needs a rate above 0 kg/s", "substance: must be one of k1-liquid, gas-low-reactivity, gas-average-high-reactivity (PB Table 4.7 classifies reactivity)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table for a release type the table does not have. The engine reads nothing as a default type. The rate message and the substance message are real engine text for two other faults: a continuous release with no rate and a substance class the table does not have.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'An `lsirTransect` call lists its distances as 0, 50 and then 50 again. What does the engine return?', '["The LSIR at 0 m and 50 m, the repeat dropped", "distancesM[2]: distances must increase strictly", "distancesM: must hold at least two distances", "scenarios[0].fatalityProbabilities: ''fire'' needs one probability of death per distance (2)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table, naming the third distance, `distancesM[2]`, the first that fails to increase. The engine drops nothing and adds no note, and the length of the list is fine here. The message about one probability of death per distance is real engine text for a scenario one probability short.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'The delayed ignition input is left out of `flammableReleaseEventTree`. Which message comes back?', '["delayedIgnitionProbability: taken as 0, so the whole release that escapes immediate ignition has been counted as no ignition", "delayedIgnitionProbability: taken from Purple Book Table 4.5 for the substance class, as the preset split is taken from section 4.8", "immediateIgnitionProbability: must be given with the delayed one", "delayedIgnitionProbability: must be the probability of delayed ignition GIVEN no immediate ignition, in [0, 1]"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the engine''s own words from the refusal table, and they say in capitals that the input is conditional: GIVEN no immediate ignition. The engine invents no number, so it takes no zero and no table value for a missing input. The fault is the delayed ignition probability, which is the field the engine names.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, '`locationIndividualRisk` is called with an empty scenario list. Which field does the refusal name, and why that one?', '["The field `scenarios`, the whole list, because there is no scenario inside it to point at.", "An LSIR of 0.000000000000 per year", "The field `scenarios[0]`, the first entry", "No field: the call returns a note"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The refusal table names `scenarios` and quotes the engine: scenarios: must be a non-empty list of scenarios. With nothing in the list there is no position such as `scenarios[0]` to name. The engine never answers an empty call with zero, since a zero would read as a real LSIR, and it never swaps a refusal for a note.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Two leaves end with the spill never lit. Pooled, how often per year is that?', '["0.000063000000", "0.000022500000", "0.000085500000", "0.001900000000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two ''no fire'' leaves, 0.000063000000 in the bund and 0.000022500000 outside it, pool into 0.000085500000 per year. Either leaf alone misses the other. ''No release'' is a separate outcome, the answered alarm at 0.001900000000 per year, because nothing leaves the tank there.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Of the five overfill leaves, which is the rarest, and how rare?', '["The contained spill that ignites in the bund, at 0.000007000000 per year.", "The overtopped bund that ignites outside it, at 0.000007500000 per year.", "The overtopped bund that is never lit, at 0.000022500000 per year.", "The answered alarm, at 0.001900000000 per year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ranked by frequency the leaves run 0.000007000000 (in the bund, ignited), 0.000007500000 (overtopped, ignited), 0.000022500000 (overtopped, unlit), 0.000063000000 (in the bund, unlit) and 0.001900000000 (alarm answered), so the ignited contained spill is the rarest by a small margin over its overtopped neighbour. The answered alarm is by far the most common leaf.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'A reviewer''s overfill worksheet shows 0.000007000000 per year for the pool fire outcome. What has gone wrong?', '["Nothing: a pool fire can only happen inside a bund, so the leaf outside the bund belongs to a separate outcome altogether.", "Only the leaf ignited in the bund was read; the leaf through the overtopped bund, 0.000007500000, was left out of the pool.", "The initiating frequency was typed as 2e-3 where the tree needs it per hour.", "The path probability was used in place of the frequency, which would give a number without a unit per year."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest says reading only the first pool fire leaf gives 0.000007000000 per year, which misses the leaf through the overtopped bund. Both leaves carry the outcome ''pool fire'', so they pool into 0.000014500000. The initiating frequency of 2e-3 is per year as stated, and a path probability such as 0.003500000000 is a different number from the leaf frequency.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Is BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE absolute or relative, and how large is it?', '["Relative, 1e-9 of the largest probability in the set, so a set of small branches gets a smaller allowance than a large one.", "Absolute, 1e-6, wide enough to let a tenth of a millionth through as ordinary rounding.", "Relative, one part in ten million of the sum.", "Absolute, 1e-9."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest says BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE is 1e-9, absolute. That covers the double rounding of 0.7, 0.2 and 0.1, which sum to 0.9999999999999999, and refuses a set such as 0.4000001 and 0.6 a tenth of a millionth over one. A tolerance of 1e-6 would pass that typing error, and no relative allowance applies to a branch sum.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'An analyst counts every delayed ignition of the EREMOR release as an explosion. What explosion frequency does that tree give?', '["0.000054000000 per year, since the split is applied afterwards by the engine whatever the analyst draws.", "0.000081000000 per year, 1.500000 times the engine''s figure, since counting every delayed ignition as an explosion swaps the split.", "0.000135000000 per year, 2.500000 times the engine''s figure.", "0.000315000000 per year, the release that never ignites."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Without a split, every delayed ignition, 0.000135000000 per year, becomes an explosion, which is 2.500000 times the engine''s 0.000054000000. The engine runs the tree it is given and cannot know a branch belongs under another, so no split is applied later. 0.000081000000 is the swapped split, a different wrong build, and 0.000315000000 is the ''no ignition'' outcome.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Which EREMOR outcome follows immediate ignition, and at what frequency?', '["The flash fire, at 0.000081000000 per year, since a flash fire is the first thing to happen when a gas release lights.", "The explosion, at 0.000054000000 per year, since immediate ignition of a gas gives a blast before the release spreads.", "The jet or pool fire, at 0.000050000000 per year.", "Nothing: every ignition is delayed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Immediate ignition gives a jet or pool fire: 5e-4 per year times 0.1 is 0.000050000000 per year. The flash fire and the explosion follow DELAYED ignition of a vapour cloud, on the path through no immediate ignition, at 0.000081000000 and 0.000054000000. The tree has an immediate ignition branch of its own.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Which cell does the engine read for an instantaneous 10000 kg release of a gas of low reactivity?', '["0.04, in the medium band, since 10000 kg is the closed top edge of the middle band.", "0.09, in the large band, since the upper edge of a printed band always opens into the band above it.", "0.02, in the small band, which the engine gives an instantaneous 500 kg.", "0.065, the k1-liquid cell."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine treats both ends of the middle band as inside it, so an instantaneous 10000 kg of gas-low-reactivity reads 0.04 (medium). The large band value of 0.09 starts above that edge, at the 20000 kg row. 0.02 is the small band row for 500 kg, and 0.065 is the liquid column, which is the same at every size.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'A continuous 5 kg/s leak of gas-low-reactivity: which probability and band apply?', '["0.04, medium.", "0.02, in the small band.", "0.065, small.", "0.2, small."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine''s lookup puts a continuous 5 kg/s of gas-low-reactivity at 0.02, small. 5 kg/s lies below the middle band, which starts at 10 kg/s. 0.065 is the k1-liquid column, and 0.2 the small cell of the average to high reactivity gases.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'How does the engine''s validation record label the cells of its Table 4.5 lookups?', '["Every cell as published, since the table is printed in full in the Purple Book and needs no reading of its own.", "Every cell as checked against a second reading, which is why a capstone may ask for any lookup to be graded.", "Every cell as the engine''s own reading.", "The band-edge cells as its own reading; the interior cells as published."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest says the validation record labels the band-edge cells as the engine''s own reading and the interior cells as published. The edges are a reading because the printed band "10 - 100 kg/s" says nothing about which band its end points join. Nothing checks the table against a second reading, so every capstone states its ignition probabilities rather than asking for a lookup.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Which substance classes does `pbDirectIgnitionProbability` accept, and what does its refusal say classifies them?', '["k1-liquid, gas-low-reactivity and gas-average-high-reactivity; the refusal cites PB Table 4.7 for the reactivity classes.", "gas-low-reactivity and gas-average-high-reactivity only, since a liquid has no direct ignition probability of its own.", "k1-liquid, gas-low-reactivity and gas-average-high-reactivity; the refusal cites Table 4.5 for the classes.", "Any name, with an unknown class read as k1-liquid."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The refusal lists exactly k1-liquid, gas-low-reactivity and gas-average-high-reactivity, and its bracket says "PB Table 4.7 classifies reactivity". Table 4.5 is the ignition table the lookup reads, which is a different table. k1-liquid has its own column at 0.065, and an unknown name is refused, `constructor` included.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'On the process deck a stated probability of death of 0.5 applies to the jet or pool fire (0.000050000000 per year). What is that outcome''s share of the deck LSIR?', '["0.000050000000, its whole frequency.", "0.000037800000, which is 0.255147 of the process deck LSIR, since the jet or pool fire is weighted the same way as the explosion there.", "0.000081000000, the flash fire''s share.", "0.000025000000, which is 0.168748 of the process deck LSIR."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Half of 0.000050000000 per year is 0.000025000000, which the engine reports as 0.168748 of the deck''s 0.000148150000. Taking the whole frequency treats a one in two chance as a certain death, which overstates it twofold. 0.000081000000 belongs to the flash fire, whose stated probability of death on the deck is 1.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'In Purple Book Appendix 6.B, what effective cloud width does the source print, and what does the whole chain through the engine give?', '["86.301293 m printed; 86.2 m through the chain.", "86.2 m in both.", "86.2 m printed; 86.301293 m through the whole chain.", "361 m in both."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest''s table prints the effective cloud width as 86.2 m in the source and 86.301293 m through the engine''s whole chain. The engine rounds no step, and on the whole chain the engine computes the width itself. 361 m is R, the distance to the grid point, which enters the coverage probability beside the width.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'What weather and direction probability, PM Pphi, does Appendix 6.B use?', '["0.456, the printed coverage probability.", "0.0368, the same in the source and the engine.", "0.835, the centreline probability of death printed in the source, which the weather and direction term is taken to scale.", "1.84e-8, the step 6 frequency."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest''s table gives PM Pphi as 0.0368 printed and 0.0368 through the engine. 0.456 is the printed coverage probability Pci and 0.835 the printed centreline probability of death Pcl. 1.84e-8 per year is the product f PM Pphi carried into step 6, which already has the frequency folded in.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Working Appendix 6.B step by step from the PRINTED values, what does the digest get?', '["A coverage probability of 0.456038429735 from the printed width of 86.2 m, Pd = 0.835 x 0.456 = 0.380760, and a contribution of 7.0104e-9 per year.", "A coverage probability of 0.456574314811, Pd = 0.380294556093 and a contribution of 0.000000006997 per year.", "A coverage probability of 0.456, Pd = 0.381 and a contribution of 7e-9 per year, the printed values with nothing recomputed.", "A Pd of 0.832930245430 and a contribution of 0.000000007010 per year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'From the printed values the digest gets a coverage probability of 0.456038429735 from the printed 86.2 m, Pd = 0.835 x 0.456 = 0.380760, and a contribution of 7.0104e-9 per year. 0.456574314811, 0.380294556093 and 0.000000006997 are the whole chain''s values. The bare printed values recompute nothing, and 0.832930245430 is the whole chain''s centreline value.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Which pair gives the centreline probability of death that Appendix 6.B prints, then the value its full recomputation in the golden file reaches?', '["0.381 in the source; 0.380294556093 in the golden recomputation, which are the two values of the centreline probability of death.", "0.835 in both.", "0.456 in the source; 0.456574314811 in the golden.", "0.835 in the source; 0.832930245430 in the golden recomputation."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The digest''s golden table gives Pcl as 0.835 printed and 0.832930245430 through the whole chain. 0.381 and 0.380294556093 are the two values of Pd = Pcl Pci, after coverage. 0.456 and 0.456574314811 are the coverage probability Pci. The whole chain computes Pcl; this course starts its own steps at the probability of death onward.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'What contribution per year does the engine''s whole Appendix 6.B chain give, against the source''s printed 7e-9?', '["0.000000007010", "0.000000108000", "0.0368", "0.000000006997"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The whole chain gives 0.000000006997 per year. 0.000000007010 is step 6 through `locationIndividualRisk` with the printed Pd of 0.381, and both reproduce the printed 7e-9 to two significant figures. 0.000000108000 is an EREMOR figure that has nothing to do with the source, and 0.0368 is PM Pphi, a probability.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'In what unit, and over what range, does the engine carry an individual risk?', '["A probability from 0 to 1, since it is a chance of death.", "Per year, 0 or more.", "Per hour, above 0.", "Per working lifetime, from 0 to 1."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The units table gives individual risk per year, 0 or more, like every LSIR and IRPA the engine returns. It is built from frequencies per year, so it is a rate with no upper bound of one, never a bare probability. Hours enter only as occupancy, and the engine reports nothing per lifetime.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'Which single place dominates the EREMOR operator''s IRPA, and with what contribution?', '["The accommodation, the most hours.", "The control room, at 0.000000597717 per year, since the operator''s supervising hours count double there.", "The process deck, at 0.000016912100 per year.", "All three equally, since the IRPA averages them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The process deck contributes 0.000016912100 of the 0.000017541379 per year. The accommodation has the most hours, 2560, but the lowest LSIR, so it adds only 0.000000031562. The control room adds 0.000000597717, and nothing counts double. The IRPA is a weighted sum, never an average.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'An analyst wants to reuse the Purple Book indoor and outdoor fractions as a vulnerability factor in an IRPA. Why does the digest say that is the wrong use?', '["Those fractions are for societal risk, counting deaths in a population; the vulnerability factor for individual risk is the analyst''s own.", "The fractions are right for the IRPA, and the engine applies them by default whenever a place is marked as being indoors.", "The fractions are for the ignition table, where they set how much of a release reaches an ignition source inside a building.", "The fractions are set by the consequence course as a dose, so they are graded there."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Purple Book indoor and outdoor fractions count deaths in a population cell, which is societal risk and the next tier''s work. For one person''s IRPA no source the engine read gives a vulnerability factor, so the engine leaves the factor to the analyst and uses 1 when none is supplied. Nothing is applied automatically for a building, and the fractions play no part in the ignition table or in any dose, which belongs to the consequence course.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'What LSIR does the EREMOR transect give at 200 m?', '["0.000004500000", "0.000000020000", "0.000000000000", "0.000000600000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 200 m the stated probabilities of death are 0.004 for the jet fire and 0.02 for the explosion, and the engine returns 0.000000600000 per year. 0.000004500000 is the 150 m figure and 0.000000020000 the 300 m figure. The LSIR falls to 0.000000000000 only at 400 m, where both stated probabilities of death are zero.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Name the distances at which the line''s individual risk falls through 1e-6 and then 1e-7 per year.', '["At 126.982958 m and 187.323816 m, the two crossings nearest the release point on this transect.", "At 200 m and 300 m, the printed distances.", "At 187.323816 m and 252.680255 m.", "At 252.680255 m and 350.000000 m, the crossings further out along the transect, beyond the 200 m point."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine puts the 1e-6 crossing at 187.323816 m and the 1e-7 crossing at 252.680255 m. 126.982958 m is the 1e-5 crossing and 350.000000 m the 1e-8 crossing. A crossing is interpolated between printed distances, so it never simply takes one of them.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'Which string does the `lsirTransect` basis print, verbatim?', '["LSIR(x) = sum f_i P_i(x); contour crossings interpolated in log10(IR) between bracketing points (linear in IR when one side is 0)", "LSIR(x) = max f_i P_i(x); contour crossings taken at the nearest printed distance to each level", "LSIR(x) = sum f_i P_i(x); contour crossings interpolated linearly in IR between bracketing points throughout", "LSIR = sum f_i x Pd_i, a person present at the location all the time, outdoors and unprotected"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The digest quotes the basis verbatim: LSIR(x) = sum f_i P_i(x); contour crossings interpolated in log10(IR) between bracketing points (linear in IR when one side is 0). The LSIR at a distance is a sum and never a maximum, and interpolation is in log10 except where one side is zero. The last string is the `locationIndividualRisk` model, for one place.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'The EREMOR transect gives 0.000000020000 per year at 300 m. Where does that come from?', '["The jet fire alone, since a jet fire reaches further than an explosion along any transect the engine is given.", "The explosion alone, with a stated probability of death of 0.001; the jet fire''s is 0 there.", "Both scenarios in equal parts, since the engine shares the LSIR out evenly between the scenarios at each distance.", "A floor the engine sets so that the 1e-8 crossing can be interpolated."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 300 m the stated probabilities of death are 0 for the jet fire and 0.001 for the explosion, so the explosion at 2e-5 per year gives 0.000000020000 alone. The engine shares nothing out and sets no floor. Every probability of death is a stated input; which scenario reaches further is read from them.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'For flammable-pb-table-4.5-large-gas, the oracle''s large gas record, which figure is its jet or pool fire?', '["0.000001350000", "0.000005250000", "0.000017500000", "0.000200000000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Its four outcomes come back as 0.000017500000 (jet or pool fire), 0.000001350000 (flash fire), 0.000000900000 (explosion) and 0.000005250000 (no ignition), so the immediate ignition branch carries the largest share of this record. 0.000200000000 belongs to flammable-default, a separate record.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'What LSIR does the golden case three-scenarios return through `locationIndividualRisk`?', '["0.000148150000", "0.000000007010", "0.000858000000", "0.000303200000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns LSIR 0.000303200000 per year for three-scenarios. 0.000148150000 belongs to the EREMOR process deck, which has four outcomes and its own inputs. 0.000000007010 is the with-zero-frequency case, and 0.000858000000 is the pooled fire outcome of an event tree case.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Which golden LSIR case returns the same 0.000000007010 per year as Appendix 6.B step 6 through `locationIndividualRisk`?', '["with-zero-frequency, which also carries a scenario at a frequency of zero.", "three-scenarios, whose LSIR of 0.000303200000 per year is the Appendix 6.B contribution scaled up to three scenarios at once.", "operator-two-areas, whose IRPA of 0.000041195890 per year carries the Appendix 6.B contribution across two areas.", "zero-probability-branch."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The golden case with-zero-frequency returns LSIR 0.000000007010 per year through `locationIndividualRisk`, the same value step 6 gives with the printed probability of death of 0.381, and it shows that a scenario frequency of zero is accepted. three-scenarios returns 0.000303200000 and operator-two-areas is an IRPA case. zero-probability-branch is an event tree case.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'operator-two-areas is an IRPA record in the golden file. Which pair of figures does it give?', '["IRPA 0.000022000000 per year at an occupancy of 1.000000000000, which the full-occupancy case returns for one place.", "0.000041195890 per year, over 0.236986301370 of the year.", "IRPA 0.000017541379 per year at an occupancy of 0.497716894977, the EREMOR operator''s figures over three places.", "IRPA 0.000041195890 per year at an occupancy of 1.000000000000."]'::jsonb, 1, 'For operator-two-areas the engine returns 0.000041195890 per year with the two areas together taking 0.236986301370 of the year. The pair 0.000022000000 and 1.000000000000 belongs to full-occupancy, and 0.000017541379 with 0.497716894977 to the EREMOR operator. Two areas that fill under a quarter of the year cannot carry an occupancy of one.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'IPL credit and SIL determination belong to which course, as the seam table puts it?', '["This course, at the Associate tier, since both reduce a scenario frequency before it reaches the LSIR.", "The consequence course, H4, which also makes each probability of death that this course is handed as an input.", "The LOPA course, H3.", "The facilities courses, which the digest says also own flare radiation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The digest says LOPA, IPL credit and SIL determination belong to the LOPA course (H3), and this course names them only at the seam. The consequence course owns the probability of death, and point source flare radiation and setback distances belong to the facilities courses, so neither owns IPL credit.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Point source flare radiation and setback distances: which courses do they belong to?', '["This course, which uses the radiation from each flare as a stated probability of death at the places nearest to it.", "The consequence course, which also fixes how far each place in the plot plan sits from the release.", "The LOPA course, which credits distance as a layer of protection in the same way it credits a relief valve.", "The facilities courses."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The seam table says point source flare radiation and setback distances belong to the facilities courses, and they are never used here. This course takes a probability of death as a stated input and computes no radiation. The consequence course owns source terms and probits, which belong there; a setback is a facilities matter and no layer the LOPA course credits.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'The risk matrix and its scoring belong to which course?', '["This course, at the Associate tier, where each LSIR is placed in a cell before an IRPA can be formed from it.", "The risk and change course; this course never scores a matrix cell.", "The consequence course, which also makes each probability of death that this course is handed as a stated input.", "The LOPA course, beside IPL credit."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The digest says the risk matrix and its scoring belong to the risk and change course, and this course never scores a matrix cell. An LSIR is a frequency per year and an IRPA a weighted sum, and neither passes through a cell. The consequence course owns the probability of death, and the LOPA course owns IPL credit.'),
  ('qra', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'The EREMOR process deck LSIR is 0.000148150000 per year and the control room''s is 0.000006545000. The four outcome frequencies are the same at both places. What makes the difference?', '["The stated probabilities of death, which are far larger on the process deck than in the control room for every outcome.", "The occupancy of each place, since the control room is manned for fewer hours of the year than the process deck is.", "The distance from each place to the release, which the engine reads from the plot plan and turns into a lower frequency for the more distant place.", "The vulnerability factor, which the engine sets lower for a building than for an open deck."]'::jsonb, 0, 'An LSIR is the sum of f times Pd at one place, and with the same frequencies at both places only the stated probabilities of death differ: 0.5, 1, 0.7 and 0.3 on the process deck against 0.02, 0, 0.1 and 0.01 in the control room. An LSIR carries no occupancy, the engine reads no plot plan and changes no frequency, and a vulnerability factor is the analyst''s own and enters only the IRPA.');
